                        {
                          "String": "/ALTERNATIVE and IN VITRO TESTS/ ... Whether increased concentrations of phenylalanine cause reduced synaptic density and alter dendritic branching /was oinvestigated by treating/ primary cortical neurons differentiated for 21 days in vitro with 5 mM phenylalanine in the presence of all essential amino acids. Immunocytochemical analysis of 12 and 21 days in vitro primary neurons revealed no changes of dendritic morphology or neuronal viability but a significant difference in synaptic density, suggesting that elevated concentrations of extracellular phenylalanine cause an impairment of synaptogenesis. Although impairment of cerebral energy metabolism has been identified as an important pathophysiological principal in many diseases, respiratory chain function has not been extensively studied in PKU before. ...Whether phenylalanine inhibits respiratory chain complexes I-V /was . In vitro analysis revealed no inhibitory effect of phenylalanine on complexes I-V, but an inhibition of pyruvate kinase, a key enzyme of glycolysis, catalyzing the formation of pyruvate. Pyruvate kinase is part of the enzyme assay to investigate enzyme activity of mitochondrial complex V and it remains to be elucidated whether this finding is relevant in vivo. In conclusion, elevated concentrations of phenylalanine might be involved in mechanisms underlying impaired synaptogenesis in PKU, supporting the common therapeutic strategy to reduce phenylalanine concentrations in the brain to prevent neurodegeneration.",
